<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Vienna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1365">1365</a>–present</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> was founded in 1365 by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV of Austria</a> and is the oldest university in today's German-speaking world. A papal bull approved most faculties at the start, and a faculty of theology was added in 1384. It became one of the largest universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and a centre of medicine, philosophy and science.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna.jpg" alt="University of Vienna" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Universität Wien Vector: Lệ Xuân · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_University_of_Vienna.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The university was founded on 12 March 1365 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV, Duke of Austria</a>, and is therefore called the Alma Mater Rudolphina. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-university">Charles University</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> (1347) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-university">Jagiellonian University</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> (1364) it is the third oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> and the oldest in the contemporary German-speaking world, though Charles University was German-speaking at its founding too. Pope Urban V did not ratify the foundation as sanctioned by Rudolf, in particular regarding theology, presumably under pressure from the Holy Roman Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a>, who wished to avoid competition for Prague. The pope granted an endowment in 1365, and papal assent came in 1384, giving the university and its Faculty of Catholic Theology full status. The first university building opened in 1385.</p><p>The Rector stood at the top, the masters and doctors formed the four faculties, and students and all other members were divided into four Academic Nations, whose elected boards chose the Rector. By the mid-15th century, with the advent of Humanism, it was the biggest university of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, with more than 6,000 students.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As a papal institution the university suffered a setback during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and epidemics, economic stagnation and the first Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> sharply reduced enrolment. Emperor Ferdinand I installed the Jesuit Order there in 1551, and in 1623 Ferdinand II incorporated the Jesuit College into the university. In the mid-18th century Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> placed it under the control of the monarchy, and her successor Joseph II abolished official attire, allowed Protestants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to enrol by 1782 and made German the compulsory language of instruction the next year.</p><p>After the Revolution of 1848 the Philosophical Faculty was given equal status with Theology, Law and Medicine, and the reforms of Leopold, Count von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thun">Thun</a> und Hohenstein, brought greater academic freedom.</p><p>In the late 1920s anti-democratic and antisemitic activity among students caused turmoil, and Professor Moritz Schlick was killed by a former student on the university steps. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> of 1938 a huge number of teachers and students were dismissed for political and racial reasons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> has about 85,000 students and is one of the largest institutions of higher learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It is associated with 17 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> laureates and has been home to scholars including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erwin-schrodinger">Erwin Schrödinger</a>, Karl Popper, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Friedrich Hayek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Gregor Mendel</a>. Its library, founded in 1365, is the oldest university library in the German-speaking world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Vienna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rudolf IV founded the <b>University of Vienna</b> in 1365 and forged the Privilegium Maius to claim higher rank.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-austria">Duchy of Austria</a></li></ul></section>
